// A built-in method's ARGUMENTS can run user code. Here the argument
// replaces the field the receiver was read from, so the receiver is held
// until the method is done with it. Without that, `push` WROTE into the
// freed list.
//
// This used to open "a built-in method runs no user code itself", and that
// is no longer true: `sort` calls the element type's `cmp`, and a map keyed
// on a user type calls its `hash` and `eq` (rt.h, TypeInfo). Nothing here
// depends on the old reading -- `push` and `set` on an int-keyed map still
// run none -- but the reentrancy those three opened is a separate rule, and
// a separate answer: the receiver is marked for the length of the operation
// and changing it from inside the callback traps (corpus/core/1000-1002,
// corpus/traps/1010-1015).
//
// Evaluation is left to right: the receiver is the list `h.ps` named when
// the expression started, so the push lands in the old list, which is then
// released -- and `h.ps` is the one `f` put there.

type H { List<int> ps; }

int f(H h) {
    h.ps = [7];
    List<int> junk = [0, 0, 0, 0];
    return 1;
}

H h = H([1, 2]);
h.ps.push(f(h));
print(h.ps.size());
print(h.ps[0]);

// A map's key and value: the key is read first, and the value can replace
// the field it came from before `set` retains it.
type K { str k; Map<str, int> m; }
int g(K k) {
    k.k = "other";
    str junk = concat("jun", "k");
    return 5;
}
K k = K(concat("ke", "y"), {});
k.m.set(k.k, g(k));
print(k.m.contains("key"));
print(k.m.contains("other"));

// With nothing after it that runs code, the receiver is used as read.
h.ps.push(3);
print(h.ps.size());
